            ("p", "There are a few reasons this matters specifically for the novice to organizational transition."),
            ("p", "<b>First, it lowers the floor for who can build.</b> You don't need a data science team to stand up a working agent. A product manager, an operations lead, or a customer success rep with real process knowledge can build something useful. That matters because the people who understand a workflow best are rarely the people with engineering resources, and Copilot Studio closes that gap."),
            ("p", "<b>Second, it raises the ceiling for what \"enabled\" means.</b> An individual using ChatGPT well is enabled. An organization with a library of agents handling onboarding questions, pulling data from internal systems, and routing tickets based on real business logic is enabled in a completely different way. Copilot Studio gives you a path from one to the other without requiring a full platform rebuild."),
            ("p", "<b>Third, and this is the part leadership teams underestimate, it creates a governance layer.</b> Individual AI use is invisible to the organization. Nobody knows what prompts people are running, what data they're pasting into a chat window, or what decisions are being informed by an ungoverned tool. Agents built in Copilot Studio live inside your tenant, respect your security boundaries, and can be monitored, versioned, and improved like any other piece of software. That's the difference between AI as a shadow practice and AI as a managed capability."),
            ("h2", "Building for the organization vs. building for the individual"),
            ("p", "It's worth sitting with this distinction because it changes how you should be thinking about ROI."),
            ("p", "When you build for an individual, you're optimizing for one person's time. That's real value, but it's capped. It scales only as far as that person's workload and only as long as they're using the tool consistently."),
            ("fig", "individual-vs-organization.webp", "Building for the individual, drawn as a plant in a pot (contained, isolated), next to building for the organization, drawn as three trees whose roots connect (connected, compounding).", "The individual to organizational growth."),
            ("p", "When you build for the organization, you're optimizing for a process. A well built agent that handles first line customer questions, or triages internal support tickets, or walks a new employee through a benefits enrollment process, doesn't just save one person time. It removes friction from the system itself. It runs the same way at 9am and at midnight. It doesn't forget a step because it's a Friday afternoon. And critically, it compounds. Every improvement you make to that agent benefits every person who touches that process going forward, not just the one person who happened to build it."),
